# The solver: minimum and maximum solutions.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# The solutions of a system of Boolean equations are ordered by inclusion, so a
# system may have a least and a greatest solution. The --min and --max options
# ask solve for those, in the sense of Theorem 2.6 in Rudeanu 1974. Both have
# long spellings, --minimum and --maximum.

# Starting with the basics. Here the minimum solution is {F}:tau:
solve --min x != 1
# but there is no maximum, since x cannot be everything:
solve --max x != 1

# Neither bound need exist. In the sbf Boolean algebra, an x that is neither 0
# nor 1 has no least and no greatest such value:
solve --min x != 0:sbf && x != 1:sbf
solve --max x != 0:sbf && x != 1:sbf
# yet the system is solvable, so plain solve still answers:
solve x != 0:sbf && x != 1:sbf

# With neither option given, solve tries for a maximum solution first, then a
# minimum, then any solution at all, and returns the first one it finds.

# Bounds can be asked for on larger systems in the same way:
solve --max {a}:sbf x != 0 && {b}:sbf x ^ {a}:sbf y = 0

# Finally, a --<type> option states which Boolean algebra to solve over, for
# formulas that carry no constant to fix it. Over sbf there is no element
# strictly between 0 and 1, so this system has no minimum solution:
solve --min --sbf x != 0 && x' != 0

# Over the Tau Boolean algebra the same system is solvable, because a splitter
# can be introduced (see demo_2.1-solver.tau):
solve --tau x != 0 && x' != 0

quit
